<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Rudolf Wettstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1594">1594</a>–<a href="/years/1666">1666</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Rudolf Wettstein was mayor of Basel from 1645 and the leading Swiss diplomat of his time. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 he obtained recognition that the Swiss Confederacy was exempt from the jurisdiction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, in effect its formal independence. The achievement made him one of the most honoured figures in the history of Basel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-rudolf-wettstein.jpg" alt="Johann Rudolf Wettstein" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Hofmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bildnis_des_Johann_Rudolf_Wettstein.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Johann Rudolf Wettstein was born in Basel on 27 October 1594, the youngest of five sons of Jakob Wettstein, who had come from Russikon near Zurich and became supervisor of the Basel hospital. He left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a> at fourteen and served a chancellery apprenticeship in Yverdon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, where he learned French. In 1611 he married Anna Maria Falkner of a noble Basel family; the marriage was unhappy, and partly because of debts to her family he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1616 to serve in the Venetian army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in Basel he was elected to the city council in 1620, served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vogt">Vogt</a> of Farnsburg and then of Riehen, became senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> master in 1635 and was elected mayor in June 1645, an office he held for twenty-one years. He travelled to Münster in 1646 without an invitation, carrying only a mandate from the reformists, and received official accreditation from the Confederacy in 1647. After long mediation he obtained on 24 October 1648 the recognition of Swiss independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> renunciation of claims to govern the Swiss. In the winter of 1650/51 he and Sebastian Peregrin Zwyer went to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand III</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and won the return of goods seized from Basel merchants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the Peasants' War of 1653 Wettstein was responsible for the public execution of seven peasant leaders. In 1661 he backed the purchase of the Amerbach Cabinet collections with public money. He died in Basel on 12 April 1666 and was buried in the Barfüsser Church. He is remembered as one of the ablest Swiss politicians of his day and as an exponent of absolutist tendencies in the Confederacy. His fame grew from about 1750 and peaked during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> bridge built in 1881, a square and a fountain in Basel bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johann Rudolf Wettstein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Basel. Died in Basel. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.56,7.59&amp;year=1640" aria-label="Basel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Johann Rudolf Wettstein"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5437 6031h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5437 6031h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.56,7.59&amp;year=1640">What was here in 1640? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
